The setup is competitive screening (multiple homeowners/principals competing for handymen/agents). Homeowner payoff \(v-p\) plus competition forces every contract onto the zero-profit line \(p=v\). Handyman IR normalizes to 0 and therefore binds on this line. This yields the low-type contract maximizing \(v-v^2\), i.e., \((v_L,p_L)=(0.5,0.5)\). Any other point on the line satisfies IC (weakly for \(H\), strictly for \(L\)) and is therefore a candidate separating menu.

However, the prior \(\frac{5}{6}\) on type \(H\) admits a pooling deviation at an intermediate \(v\) whose weighted-average cost allows the deviant homeowner to break even while attracting both types. Low types strictly prefer this deviation to the candidate separating menu, so the latter is not RS-stable. The \(\theta\) parameters are either redundant (utilities already embed costs) or non-binding technological caps; neither alters the non-existence result. Thus no separating equilibrium exists.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {no separating eq. exists}

